  "description_model": {
    "contract_model": [
      "triton_argsort(keys) takes an [N, W] int64 tensor and returns (sorted_keys, perm) with sorted_keys[n,i] == keys[n, perm[n,i]] and each row non-decreasing.",
      "The tie rule is explicit: the sort must be STABLE \u2014 among equal keys, the entry with the LOWER original index must come first in the permutation.",
      "Input domain is arbitrary int64 values, including the value 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF; duplicate keys per row are expected (quantized scores).",
      "Stability is semantically load-bearing: the permutation gathers memory slots with differing contents, so tie order affects downstream values, not just cosmetics.",
      "Downstream usage implies evidence should include tied-key cases and permutation validity (perm is a permutation of range(W) per row), not only sorted-key allclose."
    ],
    "kernel_model": [
      "One Triton program per row (grid=(N,), num_warps=1); row width W padded to BLOCK_W = next_power_of_2(W); lanes with offs >= W are masked.",
      "Bitonic sort network: outer loop doubles size (size < BLOCK_W), inner loop halves stride; direction ascending = ((offs & (size << 1)) == 0), which matches the standard bitonic formulation.",
      "Compare-exchange uses an explicit total comparator: an element is 'my_is_smaller' iff key < partner_key, or (keys tied AND perm > partner_perm) \u2014 i.e. the effective total order is (key ascending, perm DESCENDING) on ties, which is the opposite of the stable (key asc, perm asc) order the contract requires.",
      "Exchange is done through global memory: each lane stores only its own slot (no cross-lane write race), then tl.debug_barrier(), then reloads its own keys/perm from memory each iteration; partner values are loaded fresh at the top of each step.",
      "Padding sentinel: out-of-range lanes load keys/perm as other=0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF; comparator result is gated by (partner < W) in should_take_partner so in-range lanes cannot take an out-of-range partner, but out-of-range lanes CAN take an in-range partner (their gate passes when partner is in range).",
      "Python wrapper clones keys (input not mutated) and initializes perm = arange(W) broadcast per row; returns (keys_out, perm).",
      "W=1 degenerates to the loop not executing (BLOCK_W=1), returning identity, which is trivially correct."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Whether tl.debug_barrier() plus the reload pattern actually guarantees visibility of a lane's store to its partner's load within one program across all Triton versions/compilers.",
      "Whether bitonic sorting networks (which are not stable in general) can even satisfy the stability contract via the total (key, perm) comparator \u2014 here the chosen comparator appears to give perm-descending ties, but an experiment with duplicate-heavy rows is needed to confirm the observable tie order.",
      "Whether the padding-lane takeover case (real key == INT64_MAX, or the other=0 perm reload for masked lanes) can actually trigger a corrupted output for some W/N, or is benign for the tested shapes."
    ],
    "risk_map": [
      "Tie-break direction: the comparator makes ties order by perm DESCENDING (line 41: (keys == p_key) & (perm > p_perm) counts as 'smaller'/earlier), so for rows with duplicate keys the permutation places the HIGHER original index first \u2014 a direct violation of the stated stability rule. This is the primary correctness risk and should be tested with rows containing duplicate keys, checking perm against a stable reference (e.g. torch.argsort stable=True or numpy kind='stable').",
      "Sentinel collision: real keys equal to 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF tie with the padding sentinel key; an out-of-range (padding) lane's should_take_partner gate only requires partner < W, so a padding lane whose comparator says it should take an in-range partner can swap sentinel/perm values into real slots \u2014 check whether rows containing INT64_MAX produce corrupted sorted_keys or invalid perm.",
      "Permutation validity: after the network, each row's perm must be a permutation of 0..W-1; in-range/padding swaps (above) or any exchange asymmetry could break this \u2014 verify set equality per row, not just sorted_keys.",
      "Cross-iteration memory visibility: correctness relies on the store -> tl.debug_barrier() -> reload pattern making each lane's fresh global write visible; if the barrier does not order these loads (or the compiler reorders), lanes could read stale partner values \u2014 probe determinism and correctness across repeated runs.",
      "Comparator asymmetry on masked lanes: out-of-range lanes reload perm with other=0 while initially holding perm=offs; mixed sentinel conventions could produce inconsistent comparator states between iterations for padded lanes.",
      "Non-power-of-two W: masked lanes participate in the bitonic network with MAX sentinel keys; confirm sortedness and stability still hold for W values like 3, 5, 7.",
      "The two should_take_partner clauses are asymmetric w.r.t. which lane swaps; a bitonic compare-exchange requires exactly one of the two partners to take the other's value \u2014 verify lanes never double-swap or no-swap for equal (key, perm) states (e.g. when both hold identical key and perm, my_is_smaller is False both sides, so no swap: check that doesn't stall progress of the network)."
    ],
    "scope_notes": [
      "In scope: per-row ascending argsort of int64 keys, arbitrary values including INT64_MAX, duplicate keys, small row widths (the source targets 'small rows'; W is a compile-time constexpr, BLOCK_W = next_power_of_2(W)).",
      "The metric for stability claims should be exact permutation comparison against a stable reference for tied rows; sorted-keys allclose alone cannot detect tie-order violations (adversarial-precision guidance on discrete-selection behavior).",
      "Evidence should cover both power-of-two and non-power-of-two W, since padding is a first-class code path.",
      "Out of scope (not demanded by contract): in-place mutation of input (wrapper clones), performance, dtype other than int64."
    ]
  },
